  "live_records": {
    "q-126096": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126096",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14220023/assets/9312e042b08ce1aae28eab8bb64aff176fc734c38184d781e348dc5ec6058bfa.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=2y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0,\\,2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
