      "source_example_id": "e-8856",
      "knowledge_point": "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid",
      "kp_id": "e-8856"
    },
    "q-126100": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126100",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14220023/assets/cc7b2e67a5137b32917d8b96e3a53f66266e3ecabc2738b2a42417e87664457b.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=-3+4y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+4y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+3 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=1,\\,3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=3.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}y(-3+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
