    "q-362710": {
      "dom_id": "step-q362710",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14220023/assets/3dbd6e325acdddddfb0bdcca5eef77fc3e75df2ccf946592780a0e1043ee1b01.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x=-\\sqrt{y}+y$ and the $y$-axis from $y=0$ to $y=1$ (shown in the picture). Using the shell method, calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated about the $x$-axis.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The region is enclosed between the $y$-axis, the curve $x=-\\sqrt{y}+y$, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, the width of the cylindrical shells is given by $f(y)=0-(-\\sqrt{y}+y)=\\sqrt{y}-y.$ Using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(\\sqrt{y}-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3/2}-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\frac{2{y}^{5/2}}{5}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =2\\pi ((\\frac{2}{5}-0)-(\\frac{1}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
