    },
    "q-352098": {
      "dom_id": "step-q352098",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14220023/assets/7d6ef1d9a58af96b51152ce354ec85c616921187ed467273e8c41c4fe93fc19c.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis (shown in the picture). Using the shell method, calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-5{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}y({y}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}({y}^{3}-5{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-5{\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}-\\frac{5}{6}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{25}-0)-\\frac{5}{6}(\\frac{1}{125}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{100}-\\frac{1}{150}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{300}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{150}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
