      "source_example_id": "e-8853",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method",
      "kp_id": "e-8853"
    },
    "q-352100": {
      "dom_id": "step-q352100",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14220023/assets/69dbf7a007980f1f1a9c2531ad398237e22d28f329afc619e05b8372afe970d4.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis (shown in the picture). Using the shell method, calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-2y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,1-2y=0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}y({y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}({y}^{3}-2{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{16}-0)-\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{32}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{80}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{320}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{160}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
